def replace_section(start,end,new):
 global s
 old=section(start,end);s=s.replace(start+'\n\n'+old,start+'\n\n'+new.strip(),1)

s=s.replace(s.splitlines()[0],'# Climate information and temporal reconciliation of subseasonal rainfall forecasts over Australia',1)
replace_section('## Abstract','Keywords: Australia; subseasonal prediction; precipitation; temporal reconciliation; climate indices; probabilistic calibration; ensemble dependence; forecast verification.', '''Climate predictors may improve rainfall forecasts without making temporal reconciliation more useful. We examine this distinction using calibrated weekly and aggregate rainfall distributions, with a factorial experiment separating climate information from additional forecast horizons. ECMWF hindcasts are assessed in four primary Australian domains, followed by geographical and second-system checks. Nested validation excludes neighbouring years from fitting. Across the primary domains, climate information changes reconciled 28-day continuous ranked probability score by −0.31% of climatological score (95% interval −0.70 to 0.06%); none of the primary comparisons passes multiplicity adjustment. A northern improvement over rainfall-only weekly aggregation is exploratory and concentrated in a few years. Adding climate information directly to the weeks captures much of this benefit. In the northern forward evaluation, raw ECMWF performs better than every calibrated alternative, and climate-informed weekly aggregation performs better than reconciliation. The raw forecast also leads when cross-validation is restricted to the same years. Variance-based reconciliation diagnostics overpredict realised gains in all primary domain-season cases. EXTENSION_ABSTRACT_V4 These results distinguish useful climate information from the additional value of combining forecast scales, and show why coherent weekly aggregation and the raw ensemble remain essential comparators.''')

replace_section('## 1 Introduction','## 2 Data and experimental design', '''Weekly rainfall forecasts support field operations, while longer accumulations inform planting and water management. The four weekly amounts and their 28-day total describe the same weather sequence and should agree when added. This property is called temporal coherence. It holds for accumulations from a dynamical ensemble member, but separate statistical calibration of each accumulation can break it.

There are two ways to produce coherent forecasts after calibration. Bottom-up aggregation sums the calibrated weekly samples. Temporal reconciliation combines those samples with direct forecasts of longer accumulations and adjusts all scales together. Both satisfy the same accounting identity. Reconciliation adds predictive value only if the direct aggregates contain useful information beyond the calibrated weeks, or if the joint adjustment corrects errors that summation alone leaves unresolved.

Temporal hierarchies and probabilistic reconciliation are established methods (Athanasopoulos et al. 2017; Jeon et al. 2019; Panagiotelis et al. 2023; Girolimetto et al. 2024). Their usefulness for rainfall depends on the forecasts being combined. Jahangir and Quilty (2022) found contrasting outcomes across Canadian basins and accumulation scales. Bellier et al. (2023) showed that multiscale precipitation postprocessing interacts with the reconstruction of temporal dependence. These studies motivate a controlled comparison of forecast information, aggregation structure and dependence, rather than an expectation that coherence will improve every score.

Australian rainfall offers contrasting settings for this comparison. The northern wet season is influenced by the monsoon and Madden–Julian Oscillation (MJO); southwestern rainfall is concentrated in the cooler months. ENSO, the Indian Ocean Dipole and the Southern Annular Mode have relationships with rainfall that vary by location and season (Risbey et al. 2009; Wheeler et al. 2009). Their indices could therefore help predict an accumulation even when individual late-lead weeks have little skill. The dynamical ensemble may already contain much of that information, however, and the same indices could improve weekly forecasts without requiring direct aggregate models.

Australian postprocessing research already combines dynamical and statistical predictors. Calibration and bridging improve seasonal forecasts in some applications (Schepen et al. 2014), while daily Bayesian postprocessing includes temporal reordering and accumulated rainfall products (Schepen et al. 2018). ACCESS-S2 provides an operational context for multiweek prediction (Wedd et al. 2022). Studies of northern rainy-season retreat, southwestern growing-season rainfall and moisture transport show why additional predictors must be evaluated for the intended place and horizon (Cowan et al. 2024; Firth et al. 2024; Reid et al. 2024).

The unresolved issue examined here is whether a gain from climate-informed aggregate forecasts is attributable to their information or to reconciliation itself. We first hold weekly distributions fixed and vary the aggregate climate predictors and the number of forecast horizons. We then allow those predictors to enter the weeks and use their sum as a coherent alternative. This comparison separates an information gain from a reconciliation gain. A forward evaluation and additional geographical checks examine whether the original retrospective findings persist outside their initial setting.

The study contributes a controlled comparison of these information pathways, a diagnosis of when covariance-based gain estimates overstate realised improvement, and an assessment of the sensitivity to evaluation years and forecast support. The aim is to identify conditions under which added complexity is useful. The main experiment retains its original primary comparisons; subsequent controls and transfer checks are reported as exploratory analyses.''')

