replace_section('### 3.6 Additional controls and robustness checks','## 4 Results', '''The additional controls test where climate information enters the model, whether intercept adjustment alone reproduces its effect, and whether results persist with permuted predictor years or individual climate groups. Three fixed permutations are diagnostic controls and do not constitute a permutation test. Matched-size ECC and historical ranks examine dependence. The full fitting rules, including the one-month lag sensitivity, are in Supplementary Note 1.

A forward evaluation uses fitting data ending in 2014 and verifies 2015 to 2019. We also compare it with cross-validation scores for these same years, so changes in evaluation period are not confused with changes in validation design. BCa and two- and three-year block intervals, annual differences and omissions of individual or multiple years assess concentration of the northern result. The five years identified from the plotted results are omitted as a post hoc stress test, not as a new estimate of population skill. A separate monthly power-map experiment is documented in Supplementary Note 2.

Four additional geographical domains and a second-system check follow the fixed calibration and reconciliation settings. Their choices precede calculation of their new scores. These extensions assess transfer; they do not enlarge the original family of primary tests. Supplementary Tables S14–S17 provide the additional diagnostics and results.''')
s=s.replace('### 3.6 Additional controls and robustness checks','### 3.5 Controls and transfer checks')

replace_section('### 4.6 Robustness and the power-map diagnostic','### 4.7 Added-information controls and forward evaluation', '''The northern H1 minus bottom-up difference remains favourable after complete factorial refitting under positive-part increments (−0.507 mm) and signed predictors (−0.493 mm), compared with −0.541 mm under the primary correction. Changing the sample size from 200 to 1000 gives differences of −0.538 to −0.527 mm across three-seed averages. Supplementary Tables S1, S5 and S14 report these sensitivities.

Matched dependence controls also retain a northern climate contrast. H1 minus H0 CRPS is −0.414 mm under 11-sample ECC (95% interval −0.837 to −0.018 mm) and −0.382 mm under matched historical ranks (−0.774 to −0.013 mm). This supports the local direction of the contrast across the two constructions. Absolute scores and interval widths differ, so the dependence models are not interchangeable. The homogeneous dependence comparison and factorial results appear in Supplementary Tables S2 and S9.

H1 improves on bottom-up in 14 of 20 northern years, and omitting any one year leaves a mean difference between −0.627 and −0.451 mm (Fig. 9). The five years identified in review, 2000, 2008, 2010, 2016 and 2019, account for 87.0% of the net difference. Omitting all five reduces the difference to −0.094 mm and changes H1 minus H0 to +0.039 mm. Omitting only the two boundary years leaves H1 minus bottom-up at −0.373 mm. Across all 15504 possible five-year omissions, the former comparison remains favourable, but its magnitude varies substantially. These are score-omission diagnostics without model refitting (Supplementary Table S15).

The northern H1-minus-bottom-up BCa interval is −0.966 to −0.174 mm, whereas H1 minus H0 includes zero under two- and three-year blocks. The domain-averaged H1-minus-bottom-up interval includes zero with three-year blocks (−1.002 to 0.011% of climatological CRPS). These checks leave the primary multiplicity-adjusted conclusion unchanged (Supplementary Table S10).

The monthly power map reduces loss in its fitting windows but increases northern and northeastern inner-application and outer scores. The fitting and application populations differ, so this diagnostic does not establish a limitation of coherent recalibration generally. Its methods, coverage changes and covariance audit are retained in Supplementary Note 2, Tables S6 and S11, and Fig. S3.''')
s=s.replace('### 4.6 Robustness and the power-map diagnostic','### 4.6 The northern gain is concentrated in a few years')

replace_section('### 4.7 Added-information controls and forward evaluation','## 5 Discussion', '''Adding climate information to the weeks lowers northern bottom-up CRPS from 21.087 to 20.723 mm. Aggregate H1 reconciliation scores 20.546 mm; its difference from climate-informed weekly aggregation is −0.177 mm (95% interval −0.394 to 0.046 mm). The interval does not establish an additional reconciliation advantage. Weekly climate aggregation scores 17.416, 11.490 and 8.739 mm in NE, SE and SW (Table 2; Supplementary Table S8).

An aggregate intercept alone barely changes northern H0. The three fixed permuted-year models score 21.066 to 21.229 mm, compared with 20.918 mm for H0 and 20.546 mm for H1 (Table 6). These controls do not reproduce the improvement, but three fitted permutations cannot quantify a null distribution. In exploratory single-group fits, DMI scores 20.423 mm and MJO 20.671 mm. Neither is clearly better than full H1 in the paired comparisons. Niño3.4 is uncertain and SAM is adverse. These results were inspected after the full model and were not used to select its predictors. One-month monthly-index lags score 20.616 mm, differing from H1 by +0.070 mm (−0.254 to 0.416 mm).

### 4.8 Forward validation changes the comparison with the raw forecast

In the northern 2015–2019 forward evaluation, raw ECMWF scores 17.847 mm, followed by climate-informed weekly aggregation at 18.225 mm, H1 at 18.639 mm, H0 at 19.134 mm and rainfall-only bottom-up at 19.282 mm (Table 5). Thus climate information improves the calibrated system internally, while every calibrated alternative remains worse than the raw forecast. The annual scores are shown in Fig. 8 and Supplementary Table S12.

The same-period comparison separates part of the validation effect from the period effect. Northern cross-validation over 2015–2019 gives H1 18.073 mm and climate-informed weekly aggregation 18.182 mm. Both still exceed raw ECMWF. Forward fitting raises H1 CRPS by 0.566 mm and weekly climate aggregation by only 0.042 mm relative to these matched-period scores (Supplementary Table S16). The calibration disadvantage is therefore not created solely by forward validation. Training size, available climate years and the short evaluation period also differ, and this comparison cannot isolate their individual contributions.

### 4.9 Transfer across geographical domains and forecast systems

The four smaller supports inside N retain substantial variation (Supplementary Table S13). H1 minus bottom-up is −0.545 mm in the southwestern cell, +0.044 mm in the southeastern cell, −1.259 mm in the northwestern cell and −0.410 mm in the northeastern cell. All cells are included, and their spatial dependence precludes treating them as independent replications.

EXTENSION_RESULTS_V4''')

replace_section('## 5 Discussion','## 6 Conclusions', '''Climate information and reconciliation make different contributions to the final forecast. In the primary experiment, aggregate climate predictors reduce northern mean CRPS, whereas adding fortnightly nodes changes little. Climate information also helps when applied directly to the weeks. That coherent weekly alternative performs better than aggregate reconciliation in the forward period. A gain over rainfall-only bottom-up therefore cannot be attributed entirely to combining forecast scales. Table 7 summarises the evidence and its strength.

The northern seasonal setting suggests plausible sources of predictability but does not identify a single physical mechanism. MJO affects wet-season rainfall occurrence and persistence (Wheeler et al. 2009; Cowan et al. 2024). The favourable DMI-only result needs a different interpretation: IOD events generally peak before summer and weaken with the arrival of the monsoon (Australian Bureau of Meteorology, undated), while the full-model gain is concentrated in December to February. With monthly indices lagged two months, the DMI may retain information about an earlier ocean state or correlated climate conditions. This is a possible explanation, not a demonstrated pathway. The post hoc single-group comparisons cannot establish an independent DMI effect or justify selecting it as a superior model.

The annual concentration reinforces this caution. Five years account for most of the northern gain over rainfall-only aggregation, and removing them reverses the climate contrast against H0. The mean comparison with bottom-up remains favourable under every five-year omission, but can become very small. Reporting both the sign and the size of this sensitivity avoids treating persistence of a sign as evidence of a stable practical benefit.

The forward results also change the practical interpretation. Raw northern ECMWF outperforms the calibrated alternatives during 2015 to 2019 under both validation designs. The forward design further worsens aggregate H1 relative to its same-period cross-validation score. This combination is consistent with sensitivity to the training climate and the evaluation period, rather than evidence that a single validation choice explains the whole discrepancy. Operational evaluation should retain the raw forecast and climate-informed weekly aggregation as comparators.

Covariance diagnostics explain why a mathematical gain estimate need not become a forecast gain. In all 16 primary domain-season cases, the realised mean-square-error reduction lies below its variance-based prediction. Mean optimism is about five percentage points, and the fitted slopes are shallow (Fig. 7). Minimum-trace theory concerns linear unbiased forecasts under a specified covariance; this study estimates covariance from finite training data, projects samples onto the nonnegative set and verifies them out of sample. The observed mismatch applies to this diagnostic and dataset. It does not demonstrate a general bias in all covariance-based reconciliation methods. Results for particular temporally aggregated autoregressive models (Neubauer and Filzmoser 2024) similarly do not determine the outcome for these rainfall distributions.

Dependence remains part of the comparison. Historical ranks produce broad northern bottom-up intervals, but the H1-minus-H0 contrast also appears under matched ECC. Thus the local climate association is not confined to the original rank template. Absolute calibration and skill still vary with sample construction (Bellier et al. 2023). The supplementary power-map example shows why its fitting and application populations should be matched before interpreting recalibration performance.

EXTENSION_DISCUSSION_V4

For forecast practice, the relevant sequence is to establish the value of calibration against the raw ensemble, test climate information within a coherent weekly forecast, and then evaluate what direct aggregates add. Australian work on SAM–rainfall relationships and moisture transport likewise emphasises the conditional value of supplementary predictors (Firth et al. 2024; Reid et al. 2024). Coherence makes products consistent across horizons; forecast verification determines whether the additional modelling improves decisions about rainfall.''')

replace_section('## 6 Conclusions','## Data availability', '''This study separates the contribution of climate information from the contribution of temporal reconciliation. None of the three primary domain-averaged comparisons passes multiplicity adjustment. The favourable northern result is exploratory, concentrated in summer and in a few years. Much of its information benefit is also available through climate-informed weekly calibration and summation.

Forward evaluation gives a clear practical qualification. In the northern domain, raw ECMWF outperforms all calibrated alternatives during 2015 to 2019, and climate-informed weekly aggregation outperforms aggregate reconciliation. Raw ECMWF also leads over those same years under cross-validation. Thus improvements within a calibrated system do not establish an improvement over the dynamical forecast.

EXTENSION_CONCLUSION_V4

The covariance diagnostic is systematically optimistic within the evaluated domain-season cases. Together with the annual and geographical sensitivities, this finding supports evaluating added aggregate information against coherent alternatives using the same predictors. Temporal consistency is an accounting property; its predictive benefit must be established for the forecast system, location and application period.''')

